The main purpose of the present project is to enable the people of Ukraine, including cultural heritage professionals and volunteers, to reinforce the protection of cultural heritage through systematic monitoring and assessment of damage, as well as effective emergency response, in the context of increasing damage to cultural heritage, while integrating the protection of cultural heritage into recovery plans. The following activities will be carried out:
- Systemized and regular monitoring of cultural property, including World Heritage properties and sites on the Tentative List, in a coordinated international manner, and verification of damage through remote and satellite imagery analysis – continuation of cooperation with UNITAR/UNOSAT;
- Undertaking of surveys and inventory of lost or damaged protected natural heritage/biodiversity areas with a twofold objective: i) definition of measures either for restoration or identification of new areas to compensate for the loss; ii) gap analysis of the natural heritage sites included in Ukraine’s Tentative List;
- Setting-up of an operational task force, composed of trained representatives of the regional administrations, to ensure national/regional/local coordination on data recording and monitoring of damage to cultural property;
- Support a trained team of local professionals including researchers and dedicated institutions to urgently document cultural sites using Geospatial Information Technologies (GIT), 3D scanning, photogrammetry and modelling, to feed national relevant registers to enhance protection of damaged and at-risk sites;
- Develop an emergency preparedness and risk mitigation plan and enhance operational protocols for the World Heritage property ‘The Historic Centre of Odesa’ to be included in its management plan;
- Implement risk preparedness and emergency response training for key professionals likely to intervene in the World Heritage property ‘The Historic Centre of Odesa’;
- Develop an urgent conservation plan for the pre-identified pilot damaged cultural building of the “The House of Scientists”;
- Develop a standard form for damage assessment of archaeological sites and urgent documentation of archaeological sites;
- Implement a first-aid and rescue campaigns at damaged cultural sites in the affected oblasts by the Kakhovka dam destruction in southern Ukraine;
- Urgent interventions and reconstruction works to reactivate the residential functions of the historic Complex for teachers of the Polytechnic in L’viv;
- Urgent consolidation and conservation works at the Skovoroda Museum in Kharkiv region;
- Set-up and technical backstopping of a ‘Recovery and Reconstruction Unit’ to advise MoCIP, national and regional stakeholders, as well as civil society and private owners, leveraging technical expertise to address the challenges posed by the reduced workforce;
- Urgent interventions, preventive conservation, stabilization and consolidation works to reactivate essential functions of built heritage and cultural institutions at-risk and critically affected by the war across Ukraine

The Ministry for Communities, Territories Development and Infrastructure of Ukraine elaborated a draft National Framework for
Programmes for the Integrated Rehabilitation of Settlements and Territories which is expected to be applied by the government and
with the support of international partners providing expertise, tools, and best practices. In 2022, to test the approach proposed in the
draft National Framework, UNECE supported Ukraine with pilot projects in two cities, Kharkiv and Mykolaiv by developing urban
master plans which would also offer a blueprint for other cities in Ukraine. The objective of the proposed project is to strengthen
national, regional and local capacities to plan sustainable urban development solutions following a balanced, evidence-based approach.
The project will provide policy advice to the government of Ukraine on the revision of its draft Framework for Programmes for the
Integrated Rehabilitation of Settlements and Territories as well as prepare for implementation of the master plans’ visions in the pilot
cities of Kharkiv and Mykolaiv. The objective of the project will be achieved by implementing the following activities:
A1.1. Revision of the draft National Framework for Programmes for the Integrated Rehabilitation of Settlements and Territories;
A1.2. Development of an online platform containing an interactive toolbox;
A2.1. A survey-based assessment of the socio-economic situation;
A2.2. Two capacity building activities for local governments to develop and implement economic development programmes;
A3.1. Formulation of pilot projects and identification of investors;
A3.2. Matchmaking with private and public financial institutions;
A4.1. Conducting ten workshops to strengthen the institutional systems and building capacity of the local governments for the
implementation of the reconstruction master plans;
A4.2. Conducting ten trainings for local technical experts to develop solutions for project preparation, implementation, operation, and
maintenance;
A4.3. Organisation of three field trips/ study tours to improve knowledge on sustainable urban planning solutions;
A4.4. Implementation of information and dissemination campaign;
A5.1. Development of an investment tracker.

UNESCO is strengthening the teacher professional development provision and capacities of 25 Teacher Training Institutions (TTIs) in Ukraine through six key areas of action:
1) Diagnostics of the current teacher professional development (TPD) delivery model (i.e., analysis of the institutional setting as well as human, financial, and infrastructural resources);
2) Strengthening national protocols, norms, and quality assurance standards of TPD course content design and delivery (online and hybrid) as well as continuous mentoring and coaching for in-service teachers;
3) Development of relevant online/hybrid TPD courses for the implementation of the NUS reform;
4) Training of up to 300 TTIs Master Trainers and at least 2,000 selected Teacher Trainers (e.g., teachers, school management, staff of the centers of professional development, mentors, or coaches);
5) Development and management of a network of mentors/ coaches and online Community of Practice to enable continued peer-support.

The activity covers two key components:
1) Procurement of a part (40%) of the needed equipment and materials to establish the infrastructure of the Cultural Heritage Management System (CHMS);
2) Technical assistant for the Ministry of Culture and Information Policy of Ukraine and its core team in the establishment of the CHMS (one national and one international expert).

With support by Japan, UNESCO is working throught three outcomes: 
1.	The safety of Ukrainian and international journalists and media workers inside Ukraine is reinforced, with a focus on protection, monitoring, psychological support and capacity-building; 
2.	Ukrainian media outlets and journalists continue or reinstate their operations thereby providing the population with factual, conflict-sensitive and verified information of public interest, avoiding information vacuum;
3.	Independent reporting on the Ukraine war, including crimes against journalists is supported.

The implementation of standards include:  

- Launch of the online non-discrimination course for educators to provide guidance on the harmful impact of stereotypes and means of creating a safe, diverse, and tolerant education environment (with EdCamp);      

- Scale up of Social Emotional and Ethical Learning  metholodology to equip educators in order to capacitate school children with resiliency skills, an inclusive and non-discriminatory mindset as a one of the tools of GBV prevention at schools in targeted communities (with EdCamp);

- Science Ambassadors initiative (capacitating teachers as agents of science gender sensitive promotion among youth focusing on girls) (with Molodvizh);

- SCORM-course on career guidance without gender stereotypes  to expand youth knowledge and opportunities regarding the career choice, including  STEM  and social sciences (with Beetroot).

The activity aims to work with local journalists across Ukraine to support their livelihood by:
(1) documenting and verifying the damage to cultural sites;
(2) covering the impact of new attacks on cultural sites and assets;
(3) engaging in photojournalism to report on the impact of the war on the livelihoods of cultural professionals, including artists and living heritage bearers.

Following ITU's council resolution 1408 on "Assistance and support to Ukraine for rebuilding their telecommunication sector", a series of activities are being carried out in order to: 
1. to monitor and provide regular reports on the particular needs of Ukraine in the field of telecommunications, and to prepare proposals for effective technical assistance;
2. to carry out an assessment on the impact of the war in Ukraine on ITU programmes and activities in the region and provide a report thereon;
3. to ensure adequate financial and human resources mobilization, including under the internal budget and the Information and Communication Technology Development Fund, for the implementation of the proposed actions;
4. to coordinate the activities carried out by the three Sectors of the Union in accordance with resolves above, to ensure that the Union's action in favour of Ukraine is as effective as possible, and to provide a report on the matter to the 2022 Plenipotentiary conference and to the Council meeting in 2023, and future meetings and conferences as appropriate;
5. to make contributions to the WTDC-21 and PP-22, RDF-23 to support ITU efforts to rebuild Ukraine’s telecommunication infrastructure and necessary support and technical capacity building.

Activities as of 2023 have been focused on supporting the telecommunication infrastructure reconstruction.

This project aimed at empowering Ukrainian innovators and humanitarian actors in Ukraine with AI capacities to foster human rights and gender issues. 

1. Training of AI agents in existing methods of analysis:
▪ personality type (eg DISC or MBTI);
▪ psychological state (HADS questionnaire, Mississippi scale).

2. Adaptation of these systems to the context of today's war;

3. Focus group testing and further improvement.
